1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,379
A lemonade stall cuts
its price by ten percent.

2
00:00:04,699 --> 00:00:06,085
Customers love it.

3
00:00:06,405 --> 00:00:08,923
The stall sells fifteen percent more cups.

4
00:00:09,243 --> 00:00:13,723
Then the owner counts the cash at night,
and there is less money than before.

5
00:00:14,043 --> 00:00:15,109
More cups.

6
00:00:15,429 --> 00:00:16,496
Less money.

7
00:00:16,816 --> 00:00:18,480
Nobody stole anything.

8
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,656
So where did the money go?

9
00:00:20,976 --> 00:00:25,179
Let's find out, with one small number
that every shop owner should know.

10
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:27,913
Let's meet the stall.

11
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:31,177
The table rents for six
hundred rupees a day.

12
00:00:31,497 --> 00:00:35,871
That rent is the same whether the
stall sells one cup or three hundred.

13
00:00:36,191 --> 00:00:38,559
Each cup costs eight rupees to make.

14
00:00:38,879 --> 00:00:41,524
Lemons, sugar, ice and a paper cup.

15
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:44,255
And each cup sells for twenty rupees.

16
00:00:45,475 --> 00:00:47,437
So what is left from one cup?

17
00:00:47,757 --> 00:00:50,360
Twenty rupees, minus eight to make it.

18
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:51,896
Twelve rupees.

19
00:00:52,216 --> 00:00:54,264
That twelve is not profit yet.

20
00:00:54,584 --> 00:00:56,547
It has to pay the rent first.

21
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:58,424
This number has a name.

22
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:01,219
It is called the contribution margin.

23
00:01:01,539 --> 00:01:03,672
It is what each sale contributes.

24
00:01:04,892 --> 00:01:06,727
How many cups pay the rent?

25
00:01:07,047 --> 00:01:10,183
Six hundred divided by twelve is fifty.

26
00:01:10,503 --> 00:01:14,001
The first fifty cups of the
day only pay for the table.

27
00:01:14,321 --> 00:01:18,012
Cup number fifty-one is the
first one the owner keeps.

28
00:01:18,332 --> 00:01:20,295
This point is called break-even.

29
00:01:21,515 --> 00:01:24,032
Here is the same idea as a picture.

30
00:01:24,352 --> 00:01:26,123
The blue line is money in.

31
00:01:26,443 --> 00:01:30,368
It starts at zero and climbs
twenty rupees with every cup.

32
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:32,608
The coral line is money out.

33
00:01:32,928 --> 00:01:37,707
It starts at six hundred, the rent,
and climbs eight rupees with every cup.

34
00:01:38,027 --> 00:01:40,203
The lines cross at fifty cups.

35
00:01:40,523 --> 00:01:43,339
Left of the crossing,
the stall loses money.

36
00:01:43,659 --> 00:01:47,008
Right of it, every cup adds
twelve rupees of profit.

37
00:01:48,228 --> 00:01:51,620
On a normal day, the stall
sells one hundred cups.

38
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:56,143
One hundred times twelve is one
thousand two hundred rupees.

39
00:01:56,463 --> 00:01:58,831
Take away the rent of six hundred.

40
00:01:59,151 --> 00:02:01,369
The owner keeps six hundred rupees.

41
00:02:02,589 --> 00:02:03,912
Now the discount.

42
00:02:04,232 --> 00:02:06,237
Twenty rupees becomes eighteen.

43
00:02:06,557 --> 00:02:08,179
That is ten percent off.

44
00:02:08,499 --> 00:02:10,525
But the lemons do not get cheaper.

45
00:02:10,845 --> 00:02:13,491
Each cup still costs eight rupees to make.

46
00:02:13,811 --> 00:02:17,160
So each cup now leaves
ten rupees, not twelve.

47
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:19,379
The price fell by one tenth.

48
00:02:19,699 --> 00:02:22,152
What each cup gives fell by one sixth.

49
00:02:23,372 --> 00:02:24,737
Back to our puzzle.

50
00:02:25,057 --> 00:02:30,007
At twenty rupees, one hundred cups
made six hundred rupees of profit.

51
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:35,596
At eighteen, the stall sold fifteen
percent more, one hundred and fifteen cups.

52
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:39,841
That gives one thousand one
hundred and fifty, minus the rent.

53
00:02:40,161 --> 00:02:42,295
Five hundred and fifty rupees.

54
00:02:42,615 --> 00:02:45,004
Fifty rupees less, for more work.

55
00:02:45,324 --> 00:02:49,911
To earn the same six hundred, the stall
needs one hundred and twenty cups.

56
00:02:50,231 --> 00:02:53,623
That is twenty percent more
cups, just to stand still.

57
00:02:54,843 --> 00:02:56,379
Break-even moves too.

58
00:02:56,699 --> 00:03:00,283
At twenty rupees, the lines
crossed at fifty cups.

59
00:03:00,603 --> 00:03:05,808
At eighteen, the new money line is flatter,
and the crossing slides to sixty cups.

60
00:03:06,128 --> 00:03:10,309
Ten more cups every day, before
the owner earns a single rupee.

61
00:03:11,529 --> 00:03:13,215
Here is the key idea.

62
00:03:13,535 --> 00:03:17,993
A discount is taken from the price,
but it is paid from the margin.

63
00:03:18,313 --> 00:03:21,300
Two rupees off feels small next to twenty.

64
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:23,412
It is big next to twelve.

65
00:03:24,632 --> 00:03:27,491
And the thinner the
margin, the worse it gets.

66
00:03:27,811 --> 00:03:33,464
Our stall makes cups for eight rupees, so ten
percent off needs twenty percent more cups.

67
00:03:33,784 --> 00:03:39,608
If a cup cost twelve rupees to make, the same
discount would need thirty-three percent more.

68
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:44,344
If it cost sixteen, each cup would
give just four rupees, then two.

69
00:03:44,664 --> 00:03:48,397
The stall would need to sell twice
as many cups to earn the same.

70
00:03:49,617 --> 00:03:53,287
That is why shops with thin
margins are careful with sales.

71
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:58,215
When someone says we will make it up
on volume, check the margin first.

72
00:03:58,535 --> 00:04:02,887
Often the volume needed is far
more than any discount can bring.

73
00:04:04,107 --> 00:04:08,139
Here is the same stall in the
free Rimi Lab break-even lab.

74
00:04:08,459 --> 00:04:12,896
At twenty rupees and one hundred
cups, the profit is six hundred.

75
00:04:13,216 --> 00:04:17,355
Drop the price to eighteen and
sell one hundred and fifteen cups.

76
00:04:17,675 --> 00:04:20,363
The profit falls to
five hundred and fifty.

77
00:04:20,683 --> 00:04:25,120
Push the cups to one hundred and twenty,
and you are back where you started.

78
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:28,132
So, three things to remember.

79
00:04:28,452 --> 00:04:29,177
One.

80
00:04:29,497 --> 00:04:34,319
Each sale gives its price minus its
cost, and that pays the rent first.

81
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:35,321
Two.

82
00:04:35,641 --> 00:04:39,524
A discount comes out of that
margin, not out of the price.

83
00:04:39,844 --> 00:04:40,655
Three.

84
00:04:40,975 --> 00:04:45,476
When the margin is thin, a small
discount needs a lot more sales.

85
00:04:46,696 --> 00:04:50,792
It works for a lemonade stall,
a cafe or an online shop.

86
00:04:51,112 --> 00:04:55,144
Try the break-even lab for
free at Rimi Lab, link below.

87
00:04:55,464 --> 00:05:00,904
Next time, we visit the Models lab, and
see how a computer learns from examples.

88
00:05:01,224 --> 00:05:02,355
See you then.

